 ### [Gaussian clustering and merging](8436-gaussian-clustering-and-merging.md)
-Gaussian clustering and merging is a family of LOD techniques that reduces the number of splats by grouping them into representative clusters. Unlike MIP-Splatting, which handles frequency aliasing, or CityGaussian, which uses spatial clustering for pruning, this family focuses on substitutive merging where the merged splat replaces the group entirely. It builds on 3D Gaussian Splatting LOD methods.
+Gaussian clustering and merging is a family of level-of-detail (LOD) techniques for 3D Gaussian Splatting that reduces the number of splats by grouping them into representative clusters. Unlike [MIP-Splatting](6063-mip-splatting.md), which handles frequency aliasing, or [CityGaussian / VastGaussian / DoGaussian](6143-citygaussian-vastgaussian-dogaussian.md), which use spatial clustering for pruning, this family focuses on substitutive merging where the merged splat replaces the group entirely. It builds on [3D Gaussian Splatting LOD methods](5439-3d-gaussian-splatting-lod-methods.md).
 
 ### [Iterative Gaussian Synopsis](9289-iterative-gaussian-synopsis.md)
-Iterative Gaussian Synopsis is a top-down strategy for constructing a hierarchy of LOD representations for 3DGS [1]. It begins with a full-fidelity model and progressively derives coarser levels through adaptive, learnable pruning of anchors and downsampling of hierarchical grids, ensuring coherent simplification without the error accumulation inherent in bottom-up training. Unlike bottom-up approaches (e.g., LapisGS) that build coarse layers first and add enhancements, this method derives coarser levels from the full-fidelity model, ensuring inter-layer consistency. It differs from independent per-resolution models (e.g., Scaffold-GS) by sharing a foundational codebook and grids, extracting all LODs from a single optimization.
